-- title:
--   Inertia and diamond act trivially on special-fibre components
-- statement:
--   Fix a prime $p$ and an integer $M$ with $5 \le M$ and $p \nmid M$. Let $L$ be a field of characteristic zero which is a cyclotomic extension of $\mathbb Q$ of type $\{p\}$, let $\zeta \in L$ be a primitive $p$-th root of unity, and let $K$ be an intermediate field of $L \subseteq \operatorname{LaurentSeries} L$ which (hypothesis `hK`) is [`ModularCurve.laurentBaseChange L (ModularCurve.x1FunctionField (M * p))`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L103), the $L$-subfield of $\operatorname{LaurentSeries} L$ generated by the coefficientwise image of the function field of $X_1(Mp)$ over $\mathbb Q$. Let $A$ be a discrete valuation domain with fraction field $L$ such that $p$ lies in the maximal ideal of $A$ and $\zeta$ lies in the image of $A$, equipped with an $A$-algebra structure on $K$ compatible with $L$, and let $j \in K$ be a nonzero element whose Laurent expansion is the image under [`ModularCurve.coeffEmb L`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L81) of the $q$-expansion [`ModularCurve.jq`](def/ModularCurve_X0.html#L157). All the geometry below takes place over the two-chart model [`ModularCurve.TwoChart.modelTo A ↥K j`](def/ModularCurve_TwoChartModel.html#L252), the pushout of the spectra of the finite and infinite chart algebras, with its structure morphism to $\operatorname{Spec} A$, assumed proper.
--
--   Special fibre data. Let $k$ be an algebraically closed field of characteristic $p$ which is an $A$-algebra, and let $c_1 : C_1 \to \operatorname{Spec} k$, $c_2 : C_2 \to \operatorname{Spec} k$ be proper, smooth of relative dimension $1$ and geometrically integral. Let $i_1, i_2$ be closed immersions of $C_1$, $C_2$ over $k$ into the base change of the model to $k$ such that every point of that base change lies in the image of $i_1$ or of $i_2$ (`hcover`), the scheme-theoretic intersection `pullback i₁.1 i₂.1` is reduced (`hred`) and has exactly $n$ points with $0 < n$ (`hn`, `hn0`). Let $\varepsilon$ be a section of the model over $\operatorname{Spec} A$, and $\varepsilon_1, \varepsilon_2$ sections of $c_1$, $c_2$, with $\varepsilon_1$ followed by $i_1$ equal to the base change of $\varepsilon$ to $k$ (`hε₁`); moreover the base-changed section avoids the image of $i_2$ (`hεC₂`).
--
--   Picard data. Let $D$ be a `RelativePic0Designation` for the model over $A$ (a scheme $D.P$ with a structure morphism $D.\mathrm{toBase}$ to $\operatorname{Spec} A$ and a zero section), with $D.\mathrm{toBase}$ smooth and separated (`hsm`, `hsep`), and assume $D$ represents the relative sub-Picard functor cut out by the condition `algEquivZeroCut` (fibrewise algebraic equivalence to zero) for $(\text{model}, \varepsilon)$, i.e. carries a Poincaré rigidified line bundle with the usual universal property (`hrep`). The hypotheses `hreps` and `hPk` assert that the base change $D.\mathrm{baseChange}\,k$ represents the corresponding functor for the model over $k$ with section the base change of $\varepsilon$, and that its Poincaré bundle agrees with the base change to $k$ of the Poincaré bundle of `hrep`. Similarly $D_1$, $D_2$ are designations representing the corresponding functors for $(c_1,\varepsilon_1)$ and $(c_2,\varepsilon_2)$ (`hrep₁`, `hrep₂`). A morphism $\nu_2$ over the bases from $(D.\mathrm{baseChange}\,k).\mathrm{toBase}$ to $D_2.\mathrm{toBase}$ is given, together with `hν₂`: for every $k$-scheme $t : T \to \operatorname{Spec} k$ and every $T$-point $a$ of $(D.\mathrm{baseChange}\,k).\mathrm{toBase}$, the pullback of the Poincaré bundle of $D_2$ along $a$ followed by $\nu_2$ is isomorphic to the rigidification along `rigSection c₂ t ε₂` of the pullback along `curveChange i₂.1 i₂.2 t` of the pullback along $a$ of the Poincaré bundle of `hreps`; thus $\nu_2$ implements restriction of line bundles along $i_2$.
--
--   Generic-fibre data. Algebra structures of $A$ and $L$ in $\overline{\mathbb Q} = \operatorname{AlgebraicClosure} \mathbb Q$ with a scalar tower are fixed; `hsmL`, `hgiL` assert that the base change of the model to $L$ is smooth of relative dimension $1$ and geometrically integral, and `hprL`, `hgcL` that $\operatorname{pullback.snd} D.\mathrm{toBase}\,(\operatorname{Spec} L \to \operatorname{Spec} A)$ is proper and geometrically connected. Let $M_\eta$ be a `CurveModel` over $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ of the function field [`ModularCurve.x1FunctionFieldBar (M * p)`](def/ModularCurve_X1.html#L182), and $e_\eta$ an isomorphism from $M_\eta.C$ to the geometric fibre of the model over $\overline{\mathbb Q}$, compatible with the structure morphisms (`heη`). The preimage in $M_\eta.C$ of the finite chart is nonempty, and `hMηpin` pins this identification to $q$-expansions: for every element $a$ of the finite chart algebra [`ModularCurve.TwoChart.chartAlgFin A ↥K j`](def/ModularCurve_TwoChartModel.html#L135), the element of `x1FunctionFieldBar (M * p)` obtained by transporting through `Mη.ffEquiv.symm` the germ at the generic point of the image of $a$ has Laurent expansion the image under [`ModularCurve.coeffMap (algebraMap L (AlgebraicClosure ℚ))`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L16) of the expansion of $a$. The hypothesis `hgal` says that for every $g \in \operatorname{Gal}(\overline{\mathbb Q}/\mathbb Q)$ fixing the image of $L$ pointwise and every two $\overline{\mathbb Q}$-points $x, x'$ of $M_\eta.C$ with $x'$ composed into the chart coordinate equal to $\operatorname{Spec}(g)$ followed by the corresponding composite of $x$, one has $M_\eta.\mathrm{pointEquivPlace}\,x' = \mathrm{arithmeticGalois}\,(\mathrm{x1FunctionField}(Mp))\,g \cdot M_\eta.\mathrm{pointEquivPlace}\,x$.
--
--   Hecke and Galois transport. The hypotheses `hin` (`HeckeDiamondInputsAll (M * p)`: the Hecke inputs at every prime together with existence of the diamond automorphisms for $d$ coprime to the level and of their base changes to $\overline{\mathbb Q}$) and `hcomm` (`HeckeDiamondCommuteBar (M * p)`: the Hecke and diamond generators on the geometric function field commute) are assumed. A multiplicative semiring action of $\operatorname{Gal}(L/\mathbb Q)$ on $A$ is fixed, compatible with $A \to L$ (`hΓA`). Let $G$ be a [`ModularCurve.JOneP.NeronSpecialFibreGeom p`](def/ModularCurve_JOnePGeom.html#L9): abelian groups $G.J_{0s}$, $G.J_I$, $G.J_E$, a subgroup $G.\mathrm{torus} \le G.J_{0s}$ and a surjective homomorphism $G.\mathrm{proj} : G.J_{0s} \to G.J_I \times G.J_E$ with kernel $G.\mathrm{torus}$. Bijections `pts`, `ptsI`, `ptsE` identify $G.J_{0s}$, $G.J_I$, $G.J_E$ with the $k$-sections of $(D.\mathrm{baseChange}\,k).\mathrm{toBase}$, $D_1.\mathrm{toBase}$, $D_2.\mathrm{toBase}$; `hadd`, `haddI`, `haddE` assert that each is additive in the sense that the pullback of the relevant Poincaré bundle along a sum is isomorphic to the tensor product of the pullbacks; and `hproj` asserts, for each $x \in G.J_{0s}$, that `ptsI (G.proj x).1` is `pts x` followed by `RepresentsRelSubPic.pullbackHom i₁.1 i₁.2 hε₁ hreps hrep₁.some` and `ptsE (G.proj x).2` is `pts x` followed by $\nu_2$. A bijection `gpts` identifies [`ModularCurve.JOne (M * p)`](def/ModularCurve_X1.html#L186), the degree-zero divisor class group $\mathrm{Pic}^0$ of `x1FunctionFieldBar (M * p)` over $\overline{\mathbb Q}$, with the $\overline{\mathbb Q}$-points of $D.\mathrm{toBase}$. Further, $\varphi$ assigns to each element of [`ModularCurve.HeckeAlgOne`](def/ModularCurve_X1HeckeModule.html#L16) an endomorphism of $D$ over $\operatorname{Spec} A$ and $\tau$ assigns to each $s \in \operatorname{Gal}(L/\mathbb Q)$ an endomorphism of $D.P$ semilinear over the action of $s$ on $A$, subject to: each $\varphi t$ is additive for the relative group law (`hφmul`); `gpts` intertwines the Hecke action `heckeModuleOneBar` with $\varphi$ (`hφpts`); $\tau 1$ is the identity and $\tau(ss')$ is $\tau s$ followed by $\tau s'$ (`hτ1`, `hτmul`); each $\tau s$ commutes with each $\varphi t$ (`hτφ`); `gpts` is additive for the relative group law (`hgadd`); and for $\sigma' \in \operatorname{Gal}(\overline{\mathbb Q}/\mathbb Q)$ restricting on $L$ to $s$, $\mathrm{gpts}(\sigma' \cdot x)$ equals $\operatorname{Spec}(\sigma')$ followed by $\mathrm{gpts}\,x$ followed by $\tau(s^{-1})$ (`hτpts`).
--
--   Abel–Jacobi data. The hypothesis `hDL` asserts that $D.\mathrm{baseChange}\,L$ represents the relevant functor over $L$, and `hPL` the corresponding Poincaré compatibility. A morphism $\mathrm{ajL}$ from the model over $L$ to $(D.\mathrm{baseChange}\,L).\mathrm{toBase}$ is given with: the $L$-base change of $\varepsilon$ followed by $\mathrm{ajL}$ is the zero section (`hajLε`); and the Abel–Jacobi property `hajL`, namely that for every field $K'$, every morphism $\operatorname{Spec} K' \to \operatorname{Spec} L$ and every $K'$-point $x$ of the model over $L$, the pullback of the Poincaré bundle along $x$ followed by $\mathrm{ajL}$ is isomorphic to the line bundle of the relative effective Cartier divisor of $x$ tensored with the ideal module of the divisor of the cusp section, i.e. to $\mathcal O(x - \varepsilon)$. A morphism $\mathrm{kL}$ from the $\overline{\mathbb Q}$-fibre to the $L$-fibre compatible with both projections (`hkL₁`, `hkL₂`) is given, $\mathrm{ajbar} : M_\eta.C \to D.P$ is defined as $e_\eta$ followed by $\mathrm{kL}$, $\mathrm{ajL}$ and $\operatorname{pullback.fst}$ (`hajbar`), lies over the structure morphisms (`hajbar_over`), and $\bar\varepsilon$ is a $\overline{\mathbb Q}$-point of $M_\eta.C$ lying over $\varepsilon$ (`hεbar`) with $\bar\varepsilon$ followed by $\mathrm{ajbar}$ the zero section (`hεbar_aj`). Finally `hpts_aj` asserts: for all $\overline{\mathbb Q}$-points $x$, $s$ of $M_\eta.C$ with $s$ lying over $\varepsilon$, there is a degree-zero divisor $Dv$ on `x1FunctionFieldBar (M * p)` equal to $[\,M_\eta.\mathrm{pointEquivPlace}\,x\,] - [\,M_\eta.\mathrm{pointEquivPlace}\,s\,]$ whose class satisfies $\mathrm{gpts}(\mathrm{Pic}^0\text{-class of } Dv) = x$ followed by $\mathrm{ajbar}$.
--
--   Gauss-component reading. Let $w$ be a [`ModularCurve.IntegralWeightOneForm k M`](def/ModularCurve_IgusaFunctionFieldX1.html#L16) (a weight-one form on $\Gamma_1(M)$ with an integral $q$-expansion whose reduction in $k$ is nonzero), let $\mathrm{Mdl}_1$ be a curve model over $k$ of the Igusa function field [`ModularCurve.igusaFunctionFieldX1C k M w`](def/ModularCurve_IgusaFunctionFieldX1.html#L35), and $e_1$ an isomorphism $\mathrm{Mdl}_1.C \cong C_1$ over $k$ (`he₁`), with the preimage of the finite chart nonempty. The hypothesis `hgauss₁` reads the finite chart algebra on the Gauss component through $q$-expansions: for $a$ in the finite chart algebra and power series $x, y$ over $A$ with the reduction of $y$ to $k$ nonzero and $a \cdot y = x$ as Laurent series over $L$, the corresponding element of `igusaFunctionFieldX1C k M w` has Laurent expansion the reduction of $x$ divided by the reduction of $y$. The hypothesis `hεgal` asserts Galois equivariance of the cusp section: for $s \in \operatorname{Gal}(L/\mathbb Q)$, an endomorphism $ws$ of the two-chart model semilinear over $s$, and a ring automorphism $\rho_s$ of the finite chart algebra acting on Laurent series as `coeffMap s` and compatible with $ws$ on the finite chart, one has $\varepsilon$ followed by $ws$ equal to $\operatorname{Spec}$ of the action of $s$ followed by $\varepsilon$.
--
--   Place data. Let $Pl$ be a valuation subring of $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ with $p$ a nonunit of $Pl$ (`hPl`), $\rho : A \to Pl$ with $Pl \hookrightarrow \overline{\mathbb Q}$ after $\rho$ equal to $A \to \overline{\mathbb Q}$, $O$ a subring of $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ contained in $Pl$ with $\rho_O : A \to O$ likewise compatible, and $\pi_k : Pl \to k$ a surjective ring homomorphism with $A \to k$ equal to $\pi_k \circ \rho$.
--
--   Under all of these hypotheses the conclusion is the conjunction of two statements.
--
--   First: for all $s \in \operatorname{Gal}(L/\mathbb Q)$ and all $\sigma' \in \operatorname{Gal}(\overline{\mathbb Q}/\mathbb Q)$ lying in `Pl.inertiaSubgroupIn ℚ` (the image in $\operatorname{Gal}(\overline{\mathbb Q}/\mathbb Q)$ of the inertia subgroup of $Pl$ inside its decomposition subgroup) such that $\sigma'$ acts on the image of $L$ as $s^{-1}$, for every $x \in \mathrm{JOne}(Mp)$, all $O$-points $z, z'$ of $D.\mathrm{toBase}$ (sections over $\operatorname{Spec}(\rho_O)$) and all $y, y' \in G.J_{0s}$ such that $\mathrm{gpts}\,x$ factors as $\operatorname{Spec}(O \hookrightarrow \overline{\mathbb Q})$ followed by $z$, $\mathrm{gpts}(\sigma' \cdot x)$ factors as $\operatorname{Spec}(O \hookrightarrow \overline{\mathbb Q})$ followed by $z'$, the point $\mathrm{pts}\,y$ composed into $D.P$ equals $\operatorname{Spec}(\pi_k \circ (O \hookrightarrow Pl))$ followed by $z$, and $\mathrm{pts}\,y'$ composed into $D.P$ equals $\operatorname{Spec}(\pi_k \circ (O \hookrightarrow Pl))$ followed by $z'$, and assuming further that $\mathrm{pts}\,y'$ composed into $D.P$ equals $\mathrm{pts}\,y$ composed into $D.P$ followed by $\tau s$: then $(G.\mathrm{proj}\,y').1 = (G.\mathrm{proj}\,y).1$ in $G.J_I$.
--
--   Second: for all $s \in \operatorname{Gal}(L/\mathbb Q)$ and all $b \in (\mathbb Z/p)^\times$ with $s\zeta = \zeta^{\,b}$, for every natural number $d$ coprime to $Mp$ with $d \equiv 1 \pmod M$ and $d \equiv b \pmod p$, for every $\sigma' \in \mathrm{Pl.inertiaSubgroupIn}\,\mathbb Q$ acting on the image of $L$ as $s$, for every $x \in \mathrm{JOne}(Mp)$, all $O$-points $z, z'$ of $D.\mathrm{toBase}$ and all $y, y' \in G.J_{0s}$ such that $\mathrm{gpts}\,x$ factors as $\operatorname{Spec}(O \hookrightarrow \overline{\mathbb Q})$ followed by $z$, $\mathrm{gpts}\bigl(\mathrm{diamondGen}\,d \cdot (\sigma' \cdot x)\bigr)$ — the Hecke action being `heckeModuleOneBar (M * p)` and $\mathrm{diamondGen}\,d$ the diamond generator of `HeckeAlgOne` — factors as $\operatorname{Spec}(O \hookrightarrow \overline{\mathbb Q})$ followed by $z'$, and $\mathrm{pts}\,y$, $\mathrm{pts}\,y'$ composed into $D.P$ are the reductions along $\operatorname{Spec}(\pi_k \circ (O \hookrightarrow Pl))$ of $z$, $z'$ respectively, and assuming further that $\mathrm{pts}\,y'$ composed into $D.P$ equals $\mathrm{pts}\,y$ composed into $D.P$, followed by $\varphi(\mathrm{diamondGen}\,d)$ and then by $\tau(s^{-1})$: then $(G.\mathrm{proj}\,y').2 = (G.\mathrm{proj}\,y).2$ in $G.J_E$.
--
--   This is the descent step for $X_1(Mp)$ at $p$: on reductions of $O$-points of the $\mathrm{Pic}^0$-object of the two-chart (stable) model, the semilinear Galois transport attached to an inertia element is trivial on the Gauss component's factor $J_I$, and becomes trivial on the Igusa factor $J_E$ after composing with a diamond operator $\langle d\rangle$ whose residue matches the cyclotomic character of the inertia element. It feeds the statements about the free quotient of the special fibre and its monodromy pairing used in the level-lowering argument.
